      "info": {
        "contact": {
          "name": "Oli Zimpasser",
          "url": "https://math.oglimmer.de"
        },
        "description": "A REST API to parse and resolve math functions. Written in C++ with oat++.",
        "license": {
          "name": "Apache License, Version 2.0",
          "url": "http://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0"
        },
        "servers": [
          {
            "description": "server on oglimmer.de",
            "url": "https://math.oglimmer.de"
          },
          {
            "description": "server on localhost",
            "url": "http://localhost:8000"
          }
        ],
        "title": "Mathematical Function Parser Service",
        "version": "1.0",
        "tagline": "Parse, tokenize, and evaluate math expressions with variable support via a REST API",
        "homepage": "https://math.oglimmer.de",
        "docs_url": "https://math.oglimmer.de/swagger/ui",
        "license_short": "Apache License, Version 2.0",
        "license_md": "- API is published under the [Apache License, Version 2.0](http://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0).\n- Service authored by Oli Zimpasser; attribute the upstream service when redistributing results.\n- No explicit terms-of-use are published on the API homepage; treat usage as best-effort and check the Swagger UI for the most current details.",
        "about_md": "The Mathematical Function Parser Service is a small REST API by [Oli Zimpasser](https://math.oglimmer.de) that parses and evaluates mathematical expressions. It is written in C++ on top of the [oat++](https://oatpp.io) framework and exposes its OpenAPI 3.0 contract through a [Swagger UI](https://math.oglimmer.de/swagger/ui).\n\nWhat you get from the API:\n- Evaluate an expression to a numeric result (e.g. `3+4`).\n- Tokenize an expression into typed tokens.\n- Return the abstract syntax tree (AST) for an expression.\n- A combined endpoint that returns the result, parsed expression, tokens, AST, and processing time in a single response.\n- Optional variable binding via the `x` query parameter, so expressions like `2*x+1` can be resolved against a supplied value.\n\nOperational notes: all endpoints are simple `GET` calls under `/v1/` and take the formula via the `expression` query parameter. CORS is enabled, so the service can be called directly from a browser. The community catalogue reports an average response time around 162 ms and a 100% reliability score, but no formal SLA, authentication, or rate-limit policy is documented.",
        "entity_desc": {
          "calc": "Combined endpoint that tokenizes, parses, and resolves an expression, returning the result together with the parsed form, AST, tokens, and processing time at `GET /v1/calc`.",
          "resolve": "Evaluates a math expression (with optional variable `x`) and returns the numeric result as plain text at `GET /v1/resolve`.",
          "tokenize": "Breaks an expression into its typed tokens (type and data fields) at `GET /v1/tokenize`."
        },
        "auth": false
      },
